  :adr/operational-note
  "{:action-table-is-a-png \"https://www.valueflo.ws/concepts/actions/ publishes the action behaviour table as ../../assets/vf-actions.png. Do not try to read it from the HTML — WebFetch returns the surrounding prose and the table is not in it. The machine-readable form is the TTL only.\"
    :ttl-url-trap \"https://www.valueflo.ws/specification/all_vf.html does NOT contain the action instances or the effect properties (inputOutput / onhandEffect / pairsWith are absent from it). The raw TTL lives in the codeberg *pages* repo, not the valueflows/valueflows repo: codeberg.org/valueflows/pages/raw/branch/main/assets/all_vf.TTL (the /valueflows/valueflows path 404s).\"
    :nbb-pprint \"nbb needs [clojure.pprint :as pprint] required explicitly; cljs.pprint/pprint is unresolvable.\"
    :no-github-workflows \"Neither repo has .github/workflows (ADR-2607300900). CI is the murakumo fleet gate registered in scripts/fleet-ci/gates.edn.\"
    :test-runner-floor \"Both repos register test namespaces explicitly with a minimum count and exit 2 on '0 tests ran', because auto-discovery that finds nothing exits 0.\"
    :this-adr-broke-its-own-rule \"2026-08-15, TWICE. First an earlier revision of :adr/body contained the prose 10 < 4 written with bare double quotes around the numerals; then, in the very edit that added this note, two more bare-quoted strings went in the same way and truncated the body again. The second time the check caught it before landing -- which is the actual lesson: a note in an operational-note does not prevent this, and the thing that does is reading the entity back and asserting the key count. Prefer corner brackets in prose, or escape. That TERMINATED the body string early. The reader did not error -- edn/read-string returned a map, the two stray numerals became entity keys 10 and 4, and ~1.5 KB of the body (the whole landed-work section and the next-steps list) was parsed as trailing garbage instead of text. A verification step that printed 'body chars 7810' looked entirely plausible and was accepted. This is the exact shape ADR-2608136000 describes, inside the document that cites it: the file READS while being wrong, so nothing downstream complains. The check that would have caught it is not 'does it parse' but 'does the entity have exactly the expected keys, and does the body still contain the sections it is supposed to'. Escape quotes inside an :adr/body, or use corner brackets.\"}"}]
